description:
`retryFetch` catches every exception in an empty `catch (e) {}` block and, if all 7 attempts fail, falls off the end of the function returning `undefined` with no error, log, or indication of failure. Callers that assume a `Response` object (e.g. call `.json()` or check `.ok`) will get a confusing `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` far from the real cause, or worse, silently proceed with `undefined` data. At minimum, the loop should track the last error and throw it (or a wrapping error) after exhausting retries, and transient vs. non-retryable errors (e.g. 4xx) should be distinguished.

Suggested fix:
```js
async function retryFetch(url) {
  let lastError;
  for (let i = 0; i < 7; i++) {
    try {
      return await fetch(url, { headers: { Authorization: API_KEY } });
    } catch (e) {
      lastError = e;
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}
```
